{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-88460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the product of solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{x+3}-9x\\cdot {6}^{x+3}+4\\cdot {6}^{x+3}=0?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/state.json" "file_sha256" "15cdd1c6027ecea1e992b8e184de9eb3812e197fa9b93f738f68fbe733001ff9"} {"question" "q-88460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{x+3}-9x\\cdot {6}^{x+3}+4\\cdot {6}^{x+3} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}-9x+4){6}^{x+3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}-9x+4=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{6}^{x+3}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}-9x+4=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(2x-1)(x-4)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=\\frac{1}{2},4.$\n\n- The equation ${6}^{x+3}=0$ has no solutions because ${6}^{x+3}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=\\frac{1}{2},4,$ and their product is $\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/state.json" "file_sha256" "15cdd1c6027ecea1e992b8e184de9eb3812e197fa9b93f738f68fbe733001ff9"} {"question" "q-88460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "76f82e8ab5f1b20bb9ae7c74c59b3929ee19feb7fe728b57e02b97a2490dadfd"} {"question" "q-88460" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/state.json" "file_sha256" "15cdd1c6027ecea1e992b8e184de9eb3812e197fa9b93f738f68fbe733001ff9"} {"question" "q-88460" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-6"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{8}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/state.json" "file_sha256" "15cdd1c6027ecea1e992b8e184de9eb3812e197fa9b93f738f68fbe733001ff9"} {"question" "q-88460" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/state.json" "file_sha256" "15cdd1c6027ecea1e992b8e184de9eb3812e197fa9b93f738f68fbe733001ff9"} {"question" "q-237946" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{3x}-9\\cdot {5}^{3x}=0$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/state.json" "file_sha256" "15cdd1c6027ecea1e992b8e184de9eb3812e197fa9b93f738f68fbe733001ff9"} {"question" "q-237946" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{3x}-9\\cdot {5}^{3x} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-9)\\cdot {5}^{3x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-9=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{5}^{3x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-9=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+3)(x-3)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-3,3.$\n\n- The equation ${5}^{3x}=0$ has no solutions because ${5}^{3x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-3\\,\\text{and}\\,x=3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/state.json" "file_sha256" "15cdd1c6027ecea1e992b8e184de9eb3812e197fa9b93f738f68fbe733001ff9"} {"question" "q-237946" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "76f82e8ab5f1b20bb9ae7c74c59b3929ee19feb7fe728b57e02b97a2490dadfd"} {"question" "q-237946" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/state.json" "file_sha256" "15cdd1c6027ecea1e992b8e184de9eb3812e197fa9b93f738f68fbe733001ff9"} {"question" "q-237946" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/state.json" "file_sha256" "15cdd1c6027ecea1e992b8e184de9eb3812e197fa9b93f738f68fbe733001ff9"} {"question" "q-237946" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/state.json" "file_sha256" "15cdd1c6027ecea1e992b8e184de9eb3812e197fa9b93f738f68fbe733001ff9"} {"question" "q-237946" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/state.json" "file_sha256" "15cdd1c6027ecea1e992b8e184de9eb3812e197fa9b93f738f68fbe733001ff9"} {"question" "q-264778" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x+3}-7x\\cdot {2}^{x+3}+3\\cdot {2}^{x+3}=0$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/state.json" "file_sha256" "15cdd1c6027ecea1e992b8e184de9eb3812e197fa9b93f738f68fbe733001ff9"} {"question" "q-264778" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x+3}-7x\\cdot {2}^{x+3}+3\\cdot {2}^{x+3} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}-7x+3){2}^{x+3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}-7x+3=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{2}^{x+3}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}-7x+3=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(2x-1)(x-3)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=\\frac{1}{2},3.$\n\n- The equation ${2}^{x+3}=0$ has no solutions because ${2}^{x+3}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/state.json" "file_sha256" "15cdd1c6027ecea1e992b8e184de9eb3812e197fa9b93f738f68fbe733001ff9"} {"question" "q-264778" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "76f82e8ab5f1b20bb9ae7c74c59b3929ee19feb7fe728b57e02b97a2490dadfd"} {"question" "q-264778" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/state.json" "file_sha256" "15cdd1c6027ecea1e992b8e184de9eb3812e197fa9b93f738f68fbe733001ff9"} {"question" "q-264778" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/state.json" "file_sha256" "15cdd1c6027ecea1e992b8e184de9eb3812e197fa9b93f738f68fbe733001ff9"} {"question" "q-264778" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/state.json" "file_sha256" "15cdd1c6027ecea1e992b8e184de9eb3812e197fa9b93f738f68fbe733001ff9"} {"question" "q-264778" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/state.json" "file_sha256" "15cdd1c6027ecea1e992b8e184de9eb3812e197fa9b93f738f68fbe733001ff9"} {"question" "q-264786" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{2x-5}=50\\cdot {3}^{2x-5}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/state.json" "file_sha256" "15cdd1c6027ecea1e992b8e184de9eb3812e197fa9b93f738f68fbe733001ff9"} {"question" "q-264786" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{2x-5} & =50\\cdot {3}^{2x-5} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{2x-5}-50\\cdot {3}^{2x-5} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}-50)\\cdot {3}^{2x-5} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}-50=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{3}^{2x-5}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}-50=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-25) & =0 \\\\ 2(x+5)(x-5) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-5,5.$\n\n- The equation $2=0$ is not a possible solution because it's a false statement.\n\n- The equation ${3}^{2x-5}=0$ has no solutions because ${3}^{2x-5}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-5\\,\\text{and}\\,x=5.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/state.json" "file_sha256" "15cdd1c6027ecea1e992b8e184de9eb3812e197fa9b93f738f68fbe733001ff9"} {"question" "q-264786" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "76f82e8ab5f1b20bb9ae7c74c59b3929ee19feb7fe728b57e02b97a2490dadfd"} {"question" "q-264786" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/state.json" "file_sha256" "15cdd1c6027ecea1e992b8e184de9eb3812e197fa9b93f738f68fbe733001ff9"} {"question" "q-264786" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/state.json" "file_sha256" "15cdd1c6027ecea1e992b8e184de9eb3812e197fa9b93f738f68fbe733001ff9"} {"question" "q-264786" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/state.json" "file_sha256" "15cdd1c6027ecea1e992b8e184de9eb3812e197fa9b93f738f68fbe733001ff9"} {"question" "q-264786" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/state.json" "file_sha256" "15cdd1c6027ecea1e992b8e184de9eb3812e197fa9b93f738f68fbe733001ff9"} {"question" "q-264793" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $4{x}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{2x}+6\\cdot {e}^{2x}=25x\\cdot {e}^{2x}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/state.json" "file_sha256" "15cdd1c6027ecea1e992b8e184de9eb3812e197fa9b93f738f68fbe733001ff9"} {"question" "q-264793" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{2x}+6\\cdot {e}^{2x} & =25x\\cdot {e}^{2x} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{2x}-25x\\cdot {e}^{2x}+6\\cdot {e}^{2x} & =0 \\\\ (4{x}^{2}-25x+6)\\cdot {e}^{2x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}-25x+6=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{e}^{2x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $4{x}^{2}-25x+6=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(4x-1)(x-6)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=\\frac{1}{4},x=6.$\n\n- The equation ${e}^{2x}=0$ has no solutions because ${e}^{2x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=6.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/state.json" "file_sha256" "15cdd1c6027ecea1e992b8e184de9eb3812e197fa9b93f738f68fbe733001ff9"} {"question" "q-264793" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "76f82e8ab5f1b20bb9ae7c74c59b3929ee19feb7fe728b57e02b97a2490dadfd"} {"question" "q-264793" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/state.json" "file_sha256" "15cdd1c6027ecea1e992b8e184de9eb3812e197fa9b93f738f68fbe733001ff9"} {"question" "q-264793" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/state.json" "file_sha256" "15cdd1c6027ecea1e992b8e184de9eb3812e197fa9b93f738f68fbe733001ff9"} {"question" "q-264793" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/state.json" "file_sha256" "15cdd1c6027ecea1e992b8e184de9eb3812e197fa9b93f738f68fbe733001ff9"} {"question" "q-264793" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069789/state.json" "file_sha256" "15cdd1c6027ecea1e992b8e184de9eb3812e197fa9b93f738f68fbe733001ff9"}] "usage" {} "basis" 2403 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
